---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "earth chimp protein powder",
      "earthchimp protein powder review",
      "earth chimp organic vegan protein",
      "is earth chimp third party tested",
      "earth chimp protein ingredients"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Organic Picks",
    link: "/best-organic-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Earth Chimp Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      A USDA Organic vegan shake mix from a small Irish company, with a pea, pumpkin and coconut protein blend, probiotics, fruit and monk fruit. Here is what the label says, what the brand does and does not publish about testing, and what it really costs.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Flavors and Sizes</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#heavy-metals">Heavy Metals and Prop 65</a>
      <a href="#label">What's in It?</a>
      <a href="#sweeteners">Sweeteners and Sugar</a>
      <a href="#digestion">Sensitive Stomachs</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyers">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#compare">Vs Other Organic Brands</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          No, Earth Chimp does not publish third-party test results or carry a testing seal such as NSF or Informed Choice, although buyers report that the founder emails heavy metal lab reports on request. What it does have is a USDA Organic label, about 20 g of protein in a 120-calorie serving and no gums or erythritol. This review covers the label, the testing gap, heavy metals, sweeteners, digestion and cost per 25 g.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Earth Chimp Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Earth Chimp is a well-made organic plant shake with a cleaner label than most supermarket vegan proteins: no gums, no erythritol, no stevia, no added sugar and a probiotic in every serving. It is priced fairly for an organic product at about $2.21 per 25 g of protein. Its weak point is trust paperwork: the brand does not post lab results, so on heavy metals you are taking its word, and its buyers' word, rather than reading a certificate.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want an organic vegan protein that tastes mildly sweet without stevia and you will ask the brand for a batch test before committing. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you need published heavy metal results or a sport certification, if agave fiber upsets your gut, or if you want 25 g or more of protein per scoop. For organic alternatives ranked side by side, see our <a href="/best-organic-protein-powder/">best organic protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Per the Label at the Time of Writing</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-21 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 35 g serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">120</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2.21</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein, 2 lb bag</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">None</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Published third-party test results</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Earth Chimp Flavors and Sizes, Label by Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Earth Chimp sells one product, its Organic Vegan Protein Shake Mix, in four flavors and two bag sizes, with or without a scoop. On earthchimp.com at the time of writing a 2 lb (910 g) bag cost $45.99 in any flavor and a 4 lb bag $79.99, and several variants, including Strawberry, showed as sold out. It is also sold on Amazon. We have not tried it; the cards are built from the brand's product page, its posted nutrition panel and its product guide.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Earth Chimp Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not in any way a shopper can verify. We found no certificate of analysis, lab report or testing seal on <a href="https://earthchimp.com/products/earthchimp-chocolate-vegan-protein" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Earth Chimp's product page</a>, its <a href="https://earthchimp.com/pages/about-us" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">About page</a> or its <a href="https://earthchimp.com/pages/product-guide" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">product guide</a>, and it is not listed with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice as far as we could find. The About page headline reads "Made in Dublin, Tested by Us!", but the text underneath describes taste-testing each batch, not contaminant testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The certifications Earth Chimp does claim are USDA Organic, which appears on every bag, plus "GMO-Free verified" and 100% vegan. Organic certification governs how ingredients are farmed and processed. It does not test for lead, cadmium or arsenic, which plants pick up from soil regardless of farming method, so an organic seal says nothing either way about heavy metals.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a softer kind of evidence. Several Amazon reviews that Earth Chimp displays on its own site describe emailing the founder, Dave McGeady, after Consumer Reports' protein powder coverage, and receiving heavy metal lab reports within a day or two. One buyer says the report for their batch showed "basically undetectable" lead; another says the latest independent inspection put lead below the EU limit for baby formula; a third says the company regularly pays third-party labs. We cannot verify any of that, and reviews chosen by a brand are not independent, but it suggests testing happens and the brand will share it.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest answer to "is Earth Chimp third-party tested?" is: probably, according to buyers, but not publicly. If testing matters to you, do what those buyers did. Email the address printed on the bag with your batch number and ask for the heavy metal certificate of analysis, including the lab name, the detection limits and the result per serving. A brand that answers quickly with a dated, batch-matched report deserves more trust than one with a seal and no data.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heavy-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does Earth Chimp Have Heavy Metals or a Prop 65 Warning?
          </h2>

          <p>
            We could not find any published heavy metal result for Earth Chimp, from the brand, Consumer Reports or the Clean Label Project, so nobody can give you a number. We also saw no California Proposition 65 warning on the product page or the bag images the brand shows, but online listings do not always reproduce the warnings printed for California shipments, so we cannot confirm either way.
          </p>

          <p>
            What we can say is where the risk sits. Earth Chimp is built on pea, pumpkin seed and coconut protein, and the chocolate flavor adds alkalized cocoa. Plant proteins and cocoa are the two ingredients most associated with lead and cadmium in protein powder testing. In Consumer Reports' October 2025 investigation, more than two-thirds of 23 products exceeded its 0.5 microgram daily level of concern for lead in one serving, and plant-based products averaged roughly nine times more lead than dairy-based ones, per CR.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of that means Earth Chimp is high in metals; it means a plant-and-cocoa product needs data before anyone can call it low. Buyers quoted on the brand's site say the reports they were sent showed very low lead, which, if accurate, is good news. Until the brand publishes those reports, the cautious approach is to favor Vanilla or Plain & Unsweetened over Chocolate, rotate with a dairy or egg protein rather than drinking one plant powder every day, and ask for the batch report.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pregnant buyers and parents buying for children should talk to a doctor or dietitian before relying on any plant protein daily. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide ranks brands that do publish lead data, and <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> explains what the Prop 65 label means.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in Earth Chimp Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A two-scoop, 35 g serving of Earth Chimp gives about 20 g of protein and 120 calories, and the exact figures depend on which version of the label you read. The brand's product guide lists 20 g of protein, 4 g of fiber, 3 g of sugar with none added and 2 g of fat. The Chocolate nutrition panel shown in the product photos lists 21 g of protein, 5 g of fiber, 250 mg of sodium and 6 mg of iron, a third of the daily value, with 26 servings per 2 lb bag.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per 35 g serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chocolate panel (photo)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product guide</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fiber</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Total / added sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g / 0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g / 0 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Probiotic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bacillus coagulans + enzymes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bacillus coagulans</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The ingredient lists also differ, which suggests the formula has changed. The panel in the product photos lists pea, pumpkin, sunflower and coconut protein plus a digestive enzyme blend (amylase, protease, cellulase, lactase and lipase). The current ingredient list on the brand's shop page names pea, pumpkin and coconut protein only, adds organic pea starch, and lists Bacillus coagulans without the enzymes. Both versions include organic cocoa in chocolate, a fruit blend of date, banana and baobab, agave fiber, flax, natural flavors, monk fruit extract, cinnamon and turmeric. Check the panel on the bag you receive.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bag advertises 1 billion CFU of probiotics per serving, from Bacillus coagulans, a spore-forming strain chosen because it survives storage in powder. Evidence for probiotics in protein powder is thin, so treat it as a minor extra rather than a reason to buy. The bag also carries a notice to use the product "as a food supplement only" and not for weight reduction, which is standard wording for US protein products not designed as diet meal replacements. The allergen line lists coconut and a may-contain warning for peanut, sesame and nuts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Earth Chimp is blended in its own facility at Snowdrop, near Dublin, Ireland, per the brand, which is unusual for a small brand and means it controls production directly. The company says it plants a tree for each purchase, uses recyclable pouches rather than plastic tubs, and offers a no-scoop version for repeat buyers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Earth Chimp Sweetened? Monk Fruit, Fruit and Sugar
          </h2>

          <p>
            Earth Chimp is sweetened with monk fruit extract and dried fruit, with 0 g of added sugar on the label, and there is an unsweetened flavor for people who want none. It contains no stevia, no erythritol, no sucralose and no artificial sweeteners, which the brand highlights on its packaging and in its comparisons with bigger brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fruit blend of date, banana and baobab contributes most of the 3 g of total sugar per serving. That is a small amount and is counted as naturally occurring rather than added sugar under US labeling rules. Buyers often describe the sweetness as mild, and several say it lacks the aftertaste they get from stevia or sugar-alcohol powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plain & Unsweetened is the flavor to choose if you avoid monk fruit or want to control sweetness in baking, smoothies or savory recipes. The brand does not publish a separate nutrition panel for it online, so check the bag for the exact protein and calorie figures. The chocolate ingredient list includes cinnamon and turmeric, and one reviewer said the vanilla tasted more like vanilla chai than plain vanilla, crediting the same spices.
          </p>

          <p>
            If avoiding stevia is your priority, Earth Chimp is one of the better organic options. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">protein powder without stevia</a> guide lists others, including whey options with no sweetener at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="digestion" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Earth Chimp Good for a Sensitive Stomach?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Earth Chimp suits many people who bloat on other plant proteins, because it leaves out gums, erythritol and dairy, but it is not a safe bet for everyone with IBS. The brand's comparison chart calls out xanthan and guar gum and erythritol in competing powders, and buyer reviews on its site often say it is the first vegan protein that did not upset their stomach.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient to watch is organic agave fiber, which is inulin, a fructan. Fructans are one of the main FODMAP groups, and inulin is a frequent trigger for gas and bloating in people with IBS. Pea protein, dates and a few grams of added fiber can also add up for sensitive guts. If you follow a low-FODMAP plan, Earth Chimp is not marketed as low FODMAP, and you should check it with your dietitian first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergies are simpler: it is free of dairy, soy and gluten per the brand, contains coconut, and is made where peanut, sesame and nuts may be present. One buyer with celiac disease praised it, but the brand does not cite a gluten-free certification on its product page, so people with celiac disease should ask for its gluten testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is not medical advice. Anyone with a digestive disorder, kidney disease, pregnancy or medication questions should ask a doctor or registered dietitian. If your gut is the main concern, our sibling review of <a href="/drink-wholesome-protein-powder-review/">Drink Wholesome</a> covers a brand built around low-FODMAP egg white powders with posted lab results, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">sensitive stomach</a> and <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-probiotics/">protein powder with probiotics</a> guides compare more options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
