          <p>
            That sits in the middle of the organic plant market: cheaper per gram than many premium meal-style shakes and clean-label brands, and more expensive than big-box organic tubs. The brand site advertises free US shipping above a minimum order (its banner said $49 at the time of writing), and Amazon is the other common route, so compare both before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 4 lb bag is the value pick if you know you like the flavor, though at the time of writing only Vanilla was in stock in that size on the brand site. If you are unsure, start with a 2 lb bag of Vanilla or Plain, which also keeps cocoa out of your daily intake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buyers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Do Buyers Say About Earth Chimp Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mainly praise Earth Chimp's taste and gentle digestion, and mainly complain about grit and settling. The brand's home page shows review counts of about 2,140 for Chocolate, 1,510 for Vanilla, 147 for Strawberry and 138 for Plain & Unsweetened, which reflects how much more popular the two core flavors are. We read the selection of reviews the brand displays, most of them imported from Amazon.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common praise: it is not cloyingly sweet, it has no chemical aftertaste, it blends smoothly enough for a shaker, and it does not cause the bloating buyers got from other vegan powders. Several longtime customers mention using it for years, and several mention fast, generous customer service, such as replacing a split bag with two new ones within a week.
          </p>

          <p>
            The complaints are mostly about texture. One buyer who otherwise liked it called it "pretty gritty" and said it can be chalky enough to make you cough unless you use 12 to 15 oz of liquid, and that the powder settles if you do not drink it quickly. Another found the chocolate "kinda chalky" and recommended blending. Others say a shake does not keep them full, and one disliked the chai-like spice in the vanilla. Because these are reviews the brand chose to show, expect a broader sample on Amazon to include more criticism.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Earth Chimp vs Other Organic Plant Protein Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Earth Chimp's closest rivals are Truvani, Orgain, Garden of Life and Vega, and it beats most of them on label simplicity while trailing the certified brands on testing. Its own marketing leans on independence, pointing out that Garden of Life and Orgain are owned by Nestlé and that Vega is owned by private equity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against <a href="/truvani-protein-powder-review/">Truvani</a>, which is also organic, pea-based and monk fruit sweetened, Earth Chimp adds probiotics and fruit. Truvani has faced published independent lead reports and a Prop 65 warning on its chocolate, while Earth Chimp simply has no published results either way. Against <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain</a>, it avoids erythritol and gums but costs more. Against <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life</a> Sport, which lists about 30 g of protein and is NSF Certified for Sport per the brand, it gives less protein per scoop and has no sport certification, which matters for drug-tested athletes. Our <a href="/vega-protein-powder-review/">Vega review</a> covers another widely sold plant option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against our sibling review subject, <a href="/drink-wholesome-protein-powder-review/">Drink Wholesome</a>, Earth Chimp gives more protein per dollar and is certified organic, while Drink Wholesome posts its heavy metal certificates and avoids inulin. For a meal-style vegan shake with more protein and fiber, see our <a href="/happy-viking-protein-powder-review/">Happy Viking review</a>, and for pea-heavy alternatives, our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Who Should Buy Earth Chimp, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Earth Chimp if you want an organic, vegan, gum-free protein with no stevia or erythritol, for smoothies, oats or baking, and you like supporting a small independent maker. It also suits people who found other vegan powders too sweet or too chemical tasting, and anyone who wants a probiotic and a little fiber in the same scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you need published heavy metal data before you buy, or you compete in a drug-tested sport and need NSF or Informed Sport certification. Skip it too if inulin or agave fiber bothers you, if you have a coconut allergy, or if you want 25 g or more of protein per scoop. Heavy daily users worried about metals are better served rotating between a tested whey or egg protein and a plant one.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do buy, the smart order is a 2 lb bag of Vanilla or Plain, an email to the brand asking for the batch heavy metal report, and the 4 lb bag only once both the taste and the paperwork check out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading "organic" as "tested for metals"</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                USDA Organic certifies farming and handling practices, not contaminant levels. Pea, pumpkin seed and cocoa can carry lead and cadmium from soil whether organic or not. Ask Earth Chimp for a batch-specific heavy metal report rather than treating the organic seal as proof of low metals.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Defaulting to the chocolate flavor every day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chocolate adds alkalized cocoa, the ingredient most linked to lead and cadmium in protein powders. If you drink a plant shake daily and have no published test to rely on, alternate with Vanilla or Plain & Unsweetened, or rotate in a dairy or egg protein. Plain & Unsweetened also leaves room to add your own cocoa only when you want it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the agave fiber on a low-FODMAP diet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Agave fiber is inulin, a fructan and a common IBS trigger. Earth Chimp is gum-free and erythritol-free, but it is not marketed as low FODMAP. If fructans bother you, test a single scoop first, or choose a protein designed for low-FODMAP diets, and ask your dietitian about portion size.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing it with too little liquid</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buyers who like it still describe some grit and settling. One recommends 12 to 15 oz of liquid per serving and drinking it promptly. A blender, a little banana or nut butter, or plant milk instead of water helps the texture more than shaking harder. Stir it into oats or yogurt if shakes feel gritty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting an old nutrition panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The panel in the product photos (21 g protein, sunflower protein, digestive enzymes) does not match the current ingredient list (pea, pumpkin and coconut, pea starch, no enzymes) or the product guide's 20 g. Read the label on the bag you receive, especially if you track allergens or exact protein.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Earth Chimp third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Earth Chimp does not publish third-party test results or carry a testing seal such as NSF or Informed Choice. Its "Tested by Us" claim refers to taste-testing batches. Buyer reviews shown on its site say the founder emails heavy metal lab reports on request and that they showed very low lead, but we could not verify those reports. Email the brand with your batch number to ask.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Earth Chimp protein powder healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  By label standards it is clean: USDA Organic, vegan, no gums, no erythritol, no stevia, no artificial flavors and 0 g of added sugar, with about 20 g of protein in 120 calories. It does contain natural flavors and agave fiber, and its heavy metal levels are not published. It is a reasonable daily protein for most adults, ideally rotated with others.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Earth Chimp contain stevia, erythritol or sugar?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No stevia and no erythritol: it is sweetened with monk fruit extract and dried fruit (date, banana and baobab). The label lists about 3 g of total sugar per serving with 0 g added. Plain & Unsweetened contains no sweetener, per its name, though the brand does not post a separate panel for it online.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Earth Chimp flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Going by buyer reviews and review counts, Chocolate is the most popular, with more than 2,100 reviews shown on the brand's home page, followed by Vanilla at about 1,500. Buyers often call the chocolate mild and not too sweet. Some find the vanilla spiced, like a chai. Plain suits baking and savory recipes. We have not tasted them.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the common Earth Chimp reviews and complaints?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyer reviews mostly praise the taste, the lack of artificial aftertaste, gentle digestion and responsive customer service. The common complaints are a gritty or chalky texture, powder settling if left to stand, chai-like spice in the vanilla and shakes that do not keep people full. Reviews shown on the brand site are selected by the brand, so expect more criticism elsewhere.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Where is Earth Chimp made, and who owns it?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Earth Chimp says it blends its shakes in its own facility at Snowdrop, near Dublin, Ireland, and describes itself as a small independent Irish business founded by Dave McGeady, whose email is printed on the bag. It contrasts itself with Garden of Life and Orgain, which are owned by Nestlé. The US store ships from earthchimp.com and the product is also sold on Amazon.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many servings are in a bag of Earth Chimp?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  A 2 lb (910 g) bag holds about 26 servings of two scoops (35 g), per the chocolate label, and a 4 lb bag about 52. At $45.99 and $79.99 on the brand site at the time of writing, that works out to roughly $1.77 and $1.54 per serving, or about $2.21 and $1.92 per 25 g of protein. Amazon prices and bag sizes can differ, so recheck the servings line on the listing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

